From: kiwami <kiwami@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] libretro: sound at 96 kHz

The libretro port hands RetroArch its sound at 384 kHz, eight times the
48 kHz the handheld plays: SameBoy renders eight times the samples, and
RetroArch resamples all of them down every frame. On the RG DS Plus that
work alone keeps a Game Boy Color game from 60 fps under Banana's
powersave clocks (schedutil up to 1416 MHz); Gambatte sends 32.8 kHz.
The sound chip is emulated cycle by cycle whatever the rate: the rate is
only how often a sample is written out (Core/apu.c, render()), and the
high-pass filter and the DAC's fade follow it (GB_set_sample_rate). 96 kHz
is what SameBoy's own macOS frontend uses (Cocoa/Document.m), a quarter of
the work, with an anti-aliasing margin over the 48 kHz played.

Upstream: not sent (the fork's own; see .workspace/0006-more-cores/plan.md)
---
--- a/libretro/libretro.c
+++ b/libretro/libretro.c
@@ -8,7 +8,7 @@
 #include <stdarg.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #ifndef WIIU
-#define AUDIO_FREQUENCY 384000
+#define AUDIO_FREQUENCY 96000
 #else
 /* Use the internal sample rate for the Wii U */
 #define AUDIO_FREQUENCY 48000
